commands:
cd harness && make test

design: sender groups data frames into XOR FEC generations. --fec-k defaults to 8 and --fec-k 0 disables the envelope and parity path. --fec-timeout-us defaults to 200 and closes partial generations so parity is not stranded. Receiver buffers generation state in a fixed ring controlled by --fec-gens, default 64.
choice: XOR FEC was chosen over NAK because a NAK costs a full RTT before recovery and would land directly in tail percentiles. Counter-argument: FEC costs constant bandwidth overhead and repairs only one missing frame per generation.
wire_envelope: magic u32, gen_id u32, index u16, k u16, parity_count u16, protected_len u16. sizeof(fec::Envelope) is statically asserted as 16.
mtu_check: sizeof(msg::OrderBook) is printed by sender at startup, and worst_datagram is sizeof(fec::Envelope) + sizeof(uint16_t) + sizeof(msg::OrderBook). fec.h statically asserts that worst_datagram <= 1500.
publication: data frames are emitted immediately as FEC data datagrams. Parity is emitted only after the generation closes, after the data.
recovery: receiver publishes data immediately. When exactly one data frame is missing and parity is available, it XOR-recovers [u16 original_len][frame] and publishes the reconstructed frame through dedupe.
counters: sender prints fec_parity_sent and fec_gen_timeouts. receiver prints fec_parity_received, fec_recovered, fec_unrecoverable_gens, fec_recovery_p50_ns, fec_recovery_p99_ns, fec_recovery_max_ns.
rejected_alternative: parity before data was rejected because it would add latency to the common no-loss path.
rejected_alternative: NAK-and-retransmit was rejected because recovery depends on round-trip time.
